    "turn": 1,
    "user": "Can you explain what a Jacobian is, in a few sentences?",
    "response": "The Jacobian is a matrix of all the first-order partial derivatives of a vector-valued function. It describes how a small change in the input variables affects the output variables. In multivariable calculus, the Jacobian matrix is used to perform changes of variables, and its determinant — the Jacobian determinant — is used to adjust the volume element in integrals when changing coordinates. Essentially, the Jacobian captures the local linear transformation of a function.",
